I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction profile first, then identify candidate phases and build a quantitative GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the deliverables tied to the final saved project so the CIFs, fractions, profile, and statistics are internally consistent.

The chemistry points first to wolframite-type MnWO₄, but I’m treating residual precursor/oxide phases as open possibilities. I’m now matching the observed peak set against all locally available Mn–W–O, Mn–O, and W–O reference structures before deciding how many phases to refine.

The observed peaks are fully indexed by monoclinic MnWO₄ (P2/c); no unmatched, chemically plausible second-phase peaks are evident so far. The first refinement reaches a good profile agreement, but I found an over-parameterized broadening term (perfectly correlated with an instrument-width term), so I’m removing that term and testing simpler profile/background and trace-phase alternatives before finalizing.

The clean single-phase model now uses a physically valid peak-shape function and keeps atomic coordinates fixed to the reference while refining the ambient lattice and specimen displacement; it converges at \(R_{wp}=7.29\%\). I’m doing one last trace-phase test against residual Mn₃O₄, WO₃ polymorphs, and Mn₃WO₆; any candidate must produce a positive, stable scale and a real fit improvement to be retained.

The final deliverables are assembled. I excluded the weak Mn₃O₄ trial from the modeled phase list because it was not robust enough to claim; the report records that test explicitly. I’m validating the saved project, CIF, JSON schema, and point-by-point profile consistency now.

Identified one reliably modeled crystalline phase: wolframite-type MnWO₄ (P2/c), normalized to 100 wt% of modeled crystalline material.

Final fit: Rwp 7.290%, Rp 5.221%, RBragg 4.067%, GOF 2.110. No secondary phase was reliably supported; sub-percent Mn₃O₄ or amorphous material cannot be excluded.

